    <h1>How to Livestream an Event From Your Phone</h1>
    <p class="bg-dek">MomentCast works with any app that streams H.264 and AAC over RTMPS. Your phone is the smallest rig that qualifies.</p>
    <p class="bg-meta">By <strong>Aaron Alvarez</strong>, photographer and founder &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; October 29, 2026 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 8 min read</p>
  </header>

  <div class="bg-layout">

    <!-- TABLE OF CONTENTS. Anchor IDs are stable. -->
    <details class="bg-toc" open>
      <summary>In this guide</summary>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#requirements">What your app needs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#url">Your stream URL</a></li>
        <li><a href="#android">Android: IRL Pro</a></li>
        <li><a href="#iphone">iPhone: Moblin</a></li>
        <li><a href="#vertical">Streaming vertical</a></li>
        <li><a href="#other-apps">Other apps, honestly</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rig">The phone rig</a></li>
        <li><a href="#test">Test before the event</a></li>
      </ol>
    </details>

    <article class="bg-article">

      <p>My own event rig is two cameras, a switcher, and OBS Studio on a laptop. Not every event needs that. A bautizo in a small chapel, or a backup angle in case the main rig has a bad day, is covered by a phone on a tripod. The guests on the other end get the same branded watch page either way.</p>

      <p>MomentCast doesn't care what sends the video. OBS Studio, Streamlabs Desktop, vMix, Wirecast, XSplit, a hardware encoder, or an app on your phone. If it streams H.264 video and AAC audio over RTMPS, it works. This guide covers the phone side. For the full event workflow, from creating the event to downloading the recording, read <a href="/blog/livestream-event-guide">the complete guide</a>.</p>

      <!-- QUICK REFERENCE. Verified facts only. Re-check app rows when an app updates its pricing. -->
      <div class="bg-ref">
        <div class="bg-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Current answer</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Android</td><td>IRL Pro. Free, no watermark. Verified on a Galaxy S24+.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>iPhone</td><td>Moblin. Free and open source, every feature included.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Vertical from a phone</td><td>Larix Broadcaster with a paid subscription</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Stream URL format</td><td>One line: server URL with your stream key on the end</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Video and audio</td><td>H.264 and AAC. Not H.265/HEVC.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Bitrate</td><td>4,000 to 6,000 Kbps. Never under 2,500.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Keyframe interval</td><td>2 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Data use</td><td>About 1.8 GB per hour at 4,000 Kbps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="requirements">1. What your app needs</h2>

      <p>These are the rules the video pipeline enforces, so they apply to every app on every device. Check them in the app's settings before you trust it with a ceremony.</p>

      <div class="bg-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Use</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Protocol</td><td>RTMPS (secure RTMP)</td><td>The address starts with <code>rtmps://</code>. Apps that only speak plain RTMP won't connect.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Video codec</td><td>H.264, sometimes labeled AVC</td><td>Required. Most phone apps default to H.264, but some offer HEVC. Don't pick it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Audio codec</td><td>AAC</td><td>Required, and the default in every app I've seen.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bitrate</td><td>4,000 to 6,000 Kbps</td><td>Total bitrate has to stay between 2 and 14 Mbps. Some phone apps ship with presets below 2 Mbps.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Keyframe interval</td><td>2 seconds</td><td>Must be a fixed value between 2 and 10 seconds. If your app hides this setting, its default is usually inside the range.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Resolution</td><td>1920&times;1080 at 30 fps</td><td>Full HD without punishing your upload. 60 fps doubles the work for no benefit at a wedding.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Auto-reconnect</td><td>On</td><td>Phones drop connections. The app has to reconnect on its own.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="url">2. Your stream URL</h2>

      <p>Desktop encoders like OBS ask for a server URL and a stream key in two separate boxes. Phone apps usually want them in one line. The MomentCast dashboard shows them separately, so you join them yourself.</p>

      <div class="bg-msg">
        <p class="bg-msg-label">Format</p>
        <p class="bg-msg-text" id="url-format">rtmps://push.momentcast.live:443/live/YOUR_STREAM_KEY</p>
        <button class="bg-copy" type="button" data-copy="url-format">Copy</button>
      </div>

      <p>Copy the <span class="bg-ui">RTMPS Server URL</span>, paste it in the app, then copy the <span class="bg-ui">Stream Key</span> and paste it directly after the last slash. No space between them. When I left the key off, the app called the address invalid, which was fair.</p>

      <div class="bg-callout">
        <p class="bg-callout-title">Watch for invisible spaces</p>
        <p>Copying on a phone sometimes grabs a trailing space or line break, and the app sees a different key than the one you meant. If the app won't connect and everything looks right, paste the key into a notes app first, check the ends, then copy it from there.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Every real event gets its own key, revealed when you press <span class="bg-ui">Start Streaming</span> on event day. Your free test has a permanent key of its own. Save them under clearly different names in the app, so a wedding never goes out to your private test page.</p>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="android">3. Android: IRL Pro</h2>

      <p>IRL Pro is a free streaming app built for people who broadcast from their phones. No ads and no watermark. I tested it on my own Galaxy S24+ against a real MomentCast event in October 2026, and it connected over RTMPS on the first try once the URL was right.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Install <strong>IRL Pro</strong> from the Google Play Store and allow camera and microphone access.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings &rsaquo; Connections &rsaquo; New connection</strong>. It asks for a <strong>Name</strong> and a <strong>URL</strong>. Name it after the event, then paste your combined URL from <a href="#url">section 2</a>.</li>
        <li>In <strong>Settings &rsaquo; Video</strong>, set H.264, 1920&times;1080, 30 fps, and a bitrate between 4,000 and 6,000 Kbps. If a keyframe interval setting is shown, use 2 seconds.</li>
        <li>Go back to the camera screen and start the broadcast. Open the watch page on another device and confirm you're live.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Two things to know before you commit an event to it. IRL Pro hasn't had a Play Store update since September 2024. It works today, but an app that isn't maintained can break after an Android update, which is one more reason to run a test the week of the event and not the month before. And IRL Pro streams horizontal. If you need vertical from an Android phone, see <a href="#vertical">section 5</a>.</p>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="iphone">4. iPhone: Moblin</h2>

      <p>Moblin is a free, open source streaming app for iPhone. Every feature is free. The only purchases are optional app icons that support the developer, and the developer reports collecting no data from the app. It's actively maintained, and it supports RTMPS.</p>

      <p>I don't own an iPhone, so these steps come from Moblin's own documentation rather than my hands. The details may sit in slightly different places as the app updates.</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Install <strong>Moblin</strong> from the App Store. It needs iOS 16.4 or later.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Settings &rsaquo; Streams</strong> and add a new stream. Give it a name, then paste your combined URL from <a href="#url">section 2</a>. Moblin reads the <code>rtmps://</code> at the start and picks the protocol on its own.</li>
        <li>In that stream's video settings, set the codec to <strong>H.264/AVC</strong>, the resolution to 1080p, 30 fps, and a bitrate between 4,000 and 6,000 Kbps. Moblin also lets you set the keyframe interval. Use 2 seconds.</li>
        <li>Select the stream and go live. Check the watch page on another device.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Moblin streams horizontal. Its documentation says that holding the phone upright gives you a portrait interface with landscape video, so the guests still see a wide picture.</p>

      <div class="bg-callout bg-callout--danger">
        <p class="bg-callout-title">Don't leave the app mid-ceremony</p>
        <p>Moblin keeps streaming in the background, but iOS won't let it use the camera there. Switch apps to answer a text and your guests get live audio over a frozen frame until you come back. Put the phone on Do Not Disturb and leave it alone.</p>
      </div>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <!-- #vertical is the phone-specific companion to /blog/livestream-event-guide#vertical -->
      <h2 id="vertical">5. Streaming vertical</h2>

      <p>MomentCast plays vertical video too. The watch page detects the shape of the stream and sizes the player to match, with nothing to change on the event. On a phone held upright, a vertical stream fills most of the screen.</p>

      <p>Vertical has nothing to do with phones, though. OBS Studio streams vertical from a laptop with a 1080&times;1920 canvas, and <a href="/blog/livestream-event-guide#vertical">the complete guide</a> shows how. On the phone side, the two free apps above stream horizontal only. The phone app I'd use for vertical is <strong>Larix Broadcaster</strong>, which supports portrait and landscape on both iPhone and Android. Its free version has a time limit (more in <a href="#other-apps">section 6</a>), so plan on a Larix Premium subscription for the month of the event.</p>

      <div class="bg-callout bg-callout--danger">
        <p class="bg-callout-title">Pick one orientation before you go live</p>
        <p>Lock the orientation in the app, choose horizontal or vertical before you start, and stay there. If you switch and reconnect within 5 minutes, the stream continues as the same recording, still shaped for the old orientation, and it plays back squeezed or stretched. To switch on purpose, stop, wait more than 5 minutes, and go live again.</p>
      </div>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <!-- Prices and free-tier limits below were checked October 29, 2026. Re-check before editing. -->
      <h2 id="other-apps">6. Other apps, honestly</h2>

      <p>Plenty of other apps can stream to a custom RTMPS server, and MomentCast will accept them. Several of them, though, put their own branding on the free version. On a gaming stream that's a minor annoyance. On a client's wedding it's a watermark across the vows, followed by an outro for an app the couple never heard of. Here's what to know as of October 2026.</p>

      <div class="bg-table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>App</th><th>Platforms</th><th>Free version</th><th>Paid version</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Larix Broadcaster</td><td>iPhone, Android</td><td>30 minutes clean, then 30 minutes with an overlay, then the stream stops</td><td>Larix Premium, $9.99/month or $119/year. Removes the limit and the overlay.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>PRISM Live Studio</td><td>iPhone, Android</td><td>Adds a watermark and an outro</td><td>PRISM Plus, $9.99/month or $79.99/year</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Streamlabs mobile app</td><td>iPhone, Android</td><td>Adds a watermark, ads, and an outro</td><td>Streamlabs Ultra. Some platforms report Ultra is needed for custom RTMP.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Prices are US list prices from each app's own pages and can change. Read the current terms in the app before an event, because the free test only proves the connection. It can't tell you whether an app stops itself at the one-hour mark.</p>

      <p>On a computer, the usual names all work with MomentCast: OBS Studio, Streamlabs Desktop, vMix, Wirecast, and XSplit. Hardware encoders work too, as long as they support RTMPS. Some older boxes only speak plain RTMP.</p>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="rig">7. The phone rig</h2>

      <p>The phone handles the encoding. Everything around it decides whether guests can hear the vows.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A tripod or a solid mount.</strong> Nobody holds a phone steady through a 45-minute Mass.</li>
        <li><strong>An external mic.</strong> The built-in mic hears the room, the HVAC, and the guest next to it unwrapping a mint. A wireless lavalier on the officiant or a mic near the speakers makes the biggest difference of anything on this list.</li>
        <li><strong>Power.</strong> Streaming drains a battery fast and heats the phone. Stay plugged into a power bank or an outlet, and keep the phone out of direct sun.</li>
        <li><strong>A real connection.</strong> Strong Wi-Fi or 5G with upload speed comfortably above your bitrate. Run a speed test from the spot you'll stream, not the parking lot.</li>
        <li><strong>A data plan that can take it.</strong> At 4,000 Kbps a stream uses about 1.8 GB per hour. At 6,000 Kbps, about 2.7 GB.</li>
        <li><strong>Do Not Disturb.</strong> A phone call in the middle of the ceremony is a bad time to learn how your app handles interruptions.</li>
      </ul>


      <!-- ================================================================== -->
      <h2 id="test">8. Test before the event</h2>

      <p>Every MomentCast account includes a free test stream. Open <span class="bg-ui">Test Your Setup</span> on the dashboard, press <span class="bg-ui">Start Test Session</span>, and you get a server URL, a stream key, and a watch page. Join the URL and key the same way as <a href="#url">section 2</a>, start the app, and watch yourself on a second device. Sessions run up to 15 minutes and cost no credits. Details are in <a href="/blog/livestream-event-guide#test">the complete guide</a>.</p>

      <p>Use the test to confirm the connection, the audio, and how the picture looks on a phone screen. It won't catch an app that stops itself after an hour, and it won't tell you how your phone behaves after three hours in a warm church. That part is on you and your app. Test from the venue if you can.</p>


      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="bg-cta">
        <h2>Try it with the phone in your pocket</h2>
        <p>Install one of the apps above, sign in, and run a free 15-minute test. No credit card, no credits spent.</p>
        <a href="https://app.momentcast.live" target="_blank" class="mc-btn mc-btn-primary mc-btn-lg">Run a Free Test Stream</a>
      </div>

      <!-- AUTHOR -->
      <div class="bg-author">
        <div class="bg-author-mark" aria-hidden="true">A</div>
        <p><strong>Aaron Alvarez</strong> has photographed weddings, quinceañeras, and portraits since 2015. He built MomentCast to stream his own clients' events.</p>
      </div>

    </article>
